+## V4L2 Decoder
+
+### Supported Codecs
+
+The decoders are selected by name: *c2.v4l2.avc.decoder* for H.264 and
+*c2.v4l2.hevc.decoder* for HEVC, enabled by
+*ro.vendor.v4l2_codec2.decoder.supported.h264* and *.hevc* (VP8 and VP9 follow
+the same pattern). The profiles and levels come from the device, and the
+decoder also offers the High tier of every HEVC level from 4 up, which the device
+lists by its Main tier.
+
+### Output Pixel Formats and Buffers
+
+*C2_PARAMKEY_PIXEL_FORMAT* on the output offers flexible YUV 4:2:0, P010 when
+the device lists a 10-bit profile, and *IMPLEMENTATION_DEFINED* for a client
+that renders to a Surface. A 10-bit stream reaches a Surface as P010 only when
+the usage of the Surface's consumer (*C2_PARAMKEY_OUTPUT_STREAM_USAGE*) names a
+video encoder, and as 8-bit samples otherwise.
+
+A client that decodes to byte buffers gets a block pool the component creates
+from the gralloc allocator. The device writes the guest pages of a decoded
+picture, so every block is allocated with CPU write usage, and a block that a
+consumer reads through the GPU is mapped for writing and unmapped before it is
+handed on, which transfers it to the host copy.
+
+The device states the row pitch of each plane of a picture buffer, and the
+product's allocator must lay the rows out at that pitch. The decoder refuses an
+output buffer whose pitch differs, and the encoder an input frame whose plane
+strides do, with an error that names both numbers.
+
+### Input Blocks
+
+The device refuses a coded buffer smaller than the size it negotiated. The
+component reads the largest such size from the device and sizes its input blocks
+from the picture size up to it (1 MiB to 16 MiB), and copies a block that a
+client of the block model sized smaller into a pool block.
+
+### Picture Size
+
+*C2_PARAMKEY_PICTURE_SIZE* takes any even width and height from 16 up and
+follows the size of the pictures the device decodes.
+
+### Colour
+
+The component reads the colour aspects of a stream from its SPS VUI for every
+coded video sequence. A stream that states a range without a colour description
+reports that range, with the colour description unspecified.
+
